Yes, you can use the Stream API's sorted operation to attain the same result while potentially taking advantage of parallelism when suitable. Here's how you can do it:

```java
public List<String> sortAlphabetically(List<String> words) {
    return words.stream()
            .sorted()
            .collect(Collectors.toList());
}
```

However, be aware that this variant involves creating a new List containing the sorted elements rather than sorting the original list. It means this approach may consume more memory if the list is very large.

If you wish to sort the list in place and potentially benefit from parallelism, you will need to use Java's parallel sort:

```java
public List<String> sortAlphabetically(List<String> words) {
    String[] array = words.toArray(new String[0]);
    Arrays.parallelSort(array);
    return Arrays.asList(array);
}
```

This method first converts the list to an array, sorts the array in parallel, and then converts the array back to a list. This introduces additional steps and may not be as efficient as directly sorting the list, but it could improve speed on multi-core processors for sufficiently large lists.

