You can use `ArgumentException` in .NET to signify that an argument has been passed to a function that is invalid in some way. In this case, trying to add a duplicate software to the collection would be considered as providing an "invalid" argument.

Here's an example of using `ArgumentException`:

```csharp
public void AddSoftware(Software software)
{
    if (AssignedSoftware.Contains(software))
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("This software has already been assigned to the asset.");
    }
    else
    {
        AssignedSoftware.Add(software);
    }
}
```
This is clean and communicates the error well, without needing any custom exceptions.

However, if you have many similar validation rules or if this validation is something complex, you might want to consider creating your own custom exception to make your exceptions more expressive and easily identifiable. This would make your code easier to read and maintain in the long run. Here's an example:

```csharp
public class DuplicateSoftwareException : Exception
{
    public DuplicateSoftwareException(string message) : base(message)
    {
    }
}

public void AddSoftware(Software software)
{
    if (AssignedSoftware.Contains(software))
    {
        throw new DuplicateSoftwareException("This software has already been assigned to the asset.");
    }
    else
    {
        AssignedSoftware.Add(software);
    }
}
```  

Both conventions are used in .NET community, so it's up to you to decide the best approach for your application.

