In Perl, you can use the 'splice' function to remove and replace a subset of an array. To sort the subset, you can use a custom sort function. Here is how you can rewrite the Python code in Perl:

```perl
sub custom_sort {
  my($l, $n, $m, $s) = @_;
  my @subset = @{$l}[$n..$m];
  @subset = sort { $a <=> $b } @subset;
  @subset = map { $_ => 2 } @subset;
  @subset = reverse @subset if $s eq 'desc';
  splice @{$l}, $n, $m-$n+1, @subset;
  return $l;
}
```

Please note that Perl uses zero-based indexing like Python, where index '0' refers to the first element in the array. In the provided Python function, the range for slicing the list is inclusive of the end point 'm'. In Perl, when using ranges with array slicing or the 'splice' function, the range is inclusive of both end points. So, the code '$n..$m' includes both the 'n-th' and 'm-th' elements in the array.

Also note that in Perl, it's customary to pass arrays by reference (i.e., a pointer to the array) to avoid unnecessary copying of array data. If the array is large, copying it can waste memory and slow down your program, passing by reference is more efficient. So in above Perl function we have '$l' received as a reference to an array.

