This code leverages the `filter` method to extract the odd numbers from the first list (`lst1`) and the even numbers from the second list (`lst2`). If the count of these elements matches, it returns "YES", otherwise, it returns "NO".

```javascript
const exchange = (lst1, lst2) => {
  const oddLst1 = lst1.filter(n => n % 2 !== 0);
  const evenLst2 = lst2.filter(n => n % 2 === 0);
  return oddLst1.length <= evenLst2.length ? 'YES' : 'NO';
}
```

This solution works because for every odd number in the first list, we need an even number from the second list to replace it. If we have the same number or more even numbers in the second list as odd numbers in the first, we can make all numbers in the first list even. If not, it is impossible.

Here is how you can test this function:

```javascript
console.log(exchange([1, 2, 3, 4], [1, 2, 3, 4])) // "YES"
console.log(exchange([1, 2, 3, 4], [1, 5, 3, 4])) // "NO"
