Here's a possible solution implemented in Java:

This solution splits the input by either a single space or an underscore. For each word, it capitalizes the first letter (if any) and makes every other letter lower case. It uses recursion to handle each split part and concatenate them into camel case format.

```java
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(toCamelCase("this_is_a_sample_string", true));
        System.out.println(toCamelCase("this_is_a_sample_string, and_this_is_another_string", true));
    }

    static String toCamelCase(String s, boolean first) {
        // base case: if the passed string is empty or null, that means we processed all parts
        if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) {
            return "";
        }

        // find the next space or underscore
        int nextSpace = s.indexOf(' '), nextUnderScore = s.indexOf('_');
        int nextIndex = Math.min(nextSpace != -1 ? nextSpace : s.length(), nextUnderScore != -1 ? nextUnderScore : s.length());

        // to save the results of the recursive call
        String next = "";

        // if we found a space or underscore, we split the string into two parts 
        if (nextIndex != s.length()) {
            next = toCamelCase(s.substring(nextIndex + 1), false); // we send the next part for the recursive call
            s = s.substring(0, nextIndex); // we keep the rest for us to process
        }

        return (first ? s.toLowerCase() : capitalize(s)) + next;
    }

    static String capitalize(String s) {
        if (s.length() == 0) {
            return "";
        }
        return Character.toUpperCase(s.charAt(0)) + s.substring(1).toLowerCase();
    }
}

```
