Here is a simple C++ function that would solve the problem. This function takes two singly linked lists as input, which are sorted in non-descending order, and would merge these two lists into one cohesive sequence still sorted in non-descending order.

It is assumed that the linked list is defined as below:
```c++
struct ListNode {
  int val;
  ListNode *next;
  ListNode(int x) : val(x), next(NULL) {}
};
```

Below is the implementation of the function:

```c++
ListNode* mergeTwoLists(ListNode* l1, ListNode* l2) {
    // Empty list cases
    if(!l1) return l2;
    if(!l2) return l1;

    // Define start of new list
    ListNode* head = nullptr;

    if(l1->val < l2->val) {
        head = l1;
        l1 = l1->next;
    } else {
        head = l2;
        l2 = l2->next;
    }

    // Keep track of current position
    ListNode* current = head;

    // Merge lists in ascending order
    while(l1 && l2) {
        if(l1->val < l2->val) {
            current->next = l1;
            l1 = l1->next;
        } else {
            current->next = l2;
            l2 = l2->next;
        }
        current = current->next;
    }

    // Add remaining elements
    if(l1) current->next = l1;
    if(l2) current->next = l2;

    return head;
}
```

This function uses a simple merge technique. It iteratively compares the first elements from the given sorted lists and picks the smaller one, appending it to the result list.
If one list runs out of elements, it simply appends the remaining elements from the other list. It constructs a new linked list in place, without using any new memory.

