Firstly, remember modern optimizing compilers do a decent job of optimizing your code. But if you are looking for ways to manually optimize this, check the following techniques:

In C++, often the time-critical point is not the "simple" member function execution time, but rather time spent in constructors, destructors, and assignment operators, especially when objects of classes are stored in STL containers. They can lead to multiple object constructions and destructions. To mitigate this issue, you can use move semantics if applicable. However, in the current `Book` class implementation, nothing much to do in this regard.

Consider declaring the less significant fields as `short` or `unsigned short` if feasible or necessary. You can consider adjusting the data types to suit your needs to manage the memory better.

You could use a `struct` instead of a `class` for simple data encapsulation, but that would generally not improve speed or execution performance.

Do not initialize the variables in the class declaration itself if it's not necessary. Sometimes clean construction does take some runtime.

If `title` is big and copied around often, it could slow down your code. You may want to consider using `std::string_view`. However, be careful when using `string_view` as it doesn't own the memory. 

Regarding member variable `ISBN`, it is usually represented as a `string` or `long long int`. Consider this if your application may have books from different regions/countries.

Also, bear in mind that any potential "optimization" can make code less readable, and it usually wouldn't dramatically change your speed or execution performance unless you have a significant bottleneck.

On the other hand, it's important to have getters/setters for all member variables, as direct access to member variables is typically a bad practice and can violate the principle of Encapsulation. This has nothing to do with the speed improvement but is the best practice. Here's how you can implement it:

```cpp
class Book {
private:
    std::string title;
    int pages;
    int ISBN;

public:
    // getters
    std::string getTitle() const { return title; }
    int getPages() const { return pages; }
    int getISBN() const { return ISBN; }

    // setters
    void setTitle(const std::string &_title) { title = _title; }
    void setPages(int _pages) { pages = _pages; }
    void setISBN(int _ISBN) { ISBN = _ISBN; }
};
```

Remember that premature and improper optimizations may lead to other problems. Always understand the bottlenecks of your application first before applying any optimizations.

