Browser Helper Objects (BHO) was a mechanism used by versions of Internet Explorer to add functionality to the browser, however, they not supported in IE11 and Microsoft Edge. 

With that being said, to detect DOM modifications in IE7, you can use Mutation events. However, keep in mind that Mutation Events are deprecated. Performance-wise they are slow and lead to cross-browser inconsistencies.

The MutationEvent interface provides events which are sent to notify code of changes to the structure of a DOM tree.

Here's an example:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMSubtreeModified', function(e){
  console.log('tree changed');
}, false);
```

This event will fire up every time you change something in the DOM structure.

Nevertheless, the recommendation would be to move to more modern solution if possible, like developing a Chrome extension or Firefox add-on where there are far more resources and the development experience is ultimately better. These platforms have robust APIs for listening to DOM changes (`MutationObserver` for instance):

```javascript
// select the target node
var target = document.querySelector('#some-id');

// create an observer instance
var observer = new MutationObserver(function(mutations) {
  mutations.forEach(function(mutation) {
    console.log(mutation.type);
  });
});

// configuration of the observer:
var config = { attributes: true, childList: true, characterData: true };

// pass in the target node, as well as the observer options
observer.observe(target, config);
```

Again, remember to use Mutation Observers or mutation events as a last resort because they can have bad impacts on performance.

